 has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siete-leyes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siete-leyes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siete Leyes</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siete Leyes were seven constitutional laws adopted in Mexico in 1835–1836 that replaced the federal Constitution of 1824 with a centralist system.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siete-partidas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siete-partidas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siete Partidas</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siete Partidas, or Seven-Part Code, is a law code compiled in Castile under Alfonso X, drawing on Roman and canon law.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siga</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Siga was an ancient city near the mouth of the Tafna river in north-western Algeria, the capital of the Masaesylian king Syphax in the late 3rd century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sigebert-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sigebert I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sigebert I was the Merovingian king of Austrasia from 561, when the kingdom of his father Chlothar I was divided among his sons, until 575.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-iii"><span class="enc-name">Sigebert III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sigebert III was the Merovingian king of Austrasia, the eastern Frankish kingdom, from 633 or 6